# Build products:
#   In the C original these were the root-level /ssss-combine and /ssss-split
#   binaries. Cargo builds everything (both binaries, the lib, and tests) into
#   target/ instead.
/target

# Cargo.lock is intentionally NOT ignored: this crate ships binaries (an
# application), so committing the lock file pins a reproducible dependency set.

# Generated man pages:
#   ssss.manpage.xml is the single source of truth; `make doc` generates ssss.1
#   from it via xmltoman (and copies it to the per-tool names), exactly as the
#   original build does. The generated files are artifacts, not committed.
/ssss.1
/ssss-combine.1
/ssss-split.1

# Generated HTML documentation:
/ssss.1.html
